    "summary": "Dark Star and The Eleven shine amid rough audio",
    "blurb": "The second set delivers a tightly woven Dark Star into Saint Stephen and an intense The Eleven. Jerry’s guitar cuts through with fiery improvisation while Phil holds down the low end. Standout moments include Easy Wind and Friend of the Devil despite the rough audience recording.",
    "review": "On April 24, 1970 at Mammoth Gardens in Denver, the Dead served up a classic early‑70 set that still rewards a careful listen. The first set is a pleasant mix of acoustic‑friendly numbers – I Know You Rider, Friend Of The Devil and a youthful Uncle John’s Band – but it’s the second set that truly glows. A soaring Dark Star segues into Saint Stephen and then erupts into a blistering The Eleven, with Garcia “going off” on his axe and the rhythm section driving the improvisation forward. Even though the available recordings are audience‑sourced and suffer from tinny, limited frequency response, Sir Mick’s remaster of the primary tape cleans up enough to let the band’s chemistry shine through. Easy Wind and the occasional acoustic gem add flavor, while the closing jam into “It’s A Man’s World” rounds out a show that captures the restless energy of the Dead in 1970.",
